[b]Support & Resistance Zones[/b]

[b]What it does[/b]
A detector-driven support/resistance zone engine. Independent detectors each contribute evidence for a zone — a pivot cluster, a repeatedly tested level, a compact base before a displacement move, an order block, a fair value gap, a price gap, a pair of equal highs/lows. That evidence flows into one central registry, which is the only part of the script allowed to create, merge, score or draw a zone. Detectors never draw directly, so a zone on the chart can combine evidence from several detectors at once and carry a single, honest score instead of overlapping duplicate boxes.

[b]Detectors[/b]
[list]
[*]Pivot Cluster — repeated pivots within an ATR tolerance
[*]Multiple Tests — repeated confirmed reactions at a level
[*]Base / Supply & Demand — compact range followed by a displacement exit
[*]Order Block — displacement + prior-structure break + last opposite-color origin candle
[*]Fair Value Gap and classical Price Gap, tracked as separate sources
[*]Equal High / Equal Low Liquidity — paired same-level pivots
[/list]

[b]Confluence[/b]
[list]
[*]EMA and VWAP proximity
[*]Fibonacci retracement levels
[*]Psychological (round-number) price levels
[*]Relative volume on touch
[/list]

[b]Zone lifecycle[/b]
Every zone moves through NEW -> TESTED -> CONFIRMED (on a successful reaction) or -> WEAK (if the reaction horizon expires without one). A confirmed break either retires the zone or, if enabled, flips it into a fresh zone on the opposite side. Only the strongest, nearest-to-price zones per side are ever drawn — the internal registry can hold far more than what's shown.

[b]What the score is not[/b]
The 0-100 score on each zone is a heuristic confluence/quality score built from which detectors and confluence factors agree on that price area. It is not a statistical probability and not a guarantee that price will react there.

